why 4 3/8 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and tenor fall by step together on a weak eighth note. The fifths pass between the beats, so they are hardly heard.
why 3 3/8 SPACING The alto holds a high C while the tenor walks down D–C–B–A. The gap lasts two eighths and closes as the tenor turns back up.
why 3 1/2 SPACING The widest point of that gap; the tenor climbs B–C–E right after.
why 5 1/4 RANGE The bass climbs A–B–C♯–D in a scale toward D minor, rising into the tenor's range for a beat.
why 5 1/2 RANGE The top of the climb, D; the bass walks back down C–B♭–A at once.
why 6 1/2 DOUBLING Over the bass's D the upper voices pass through G on the way to the D major chord at the fermata. D is the root of that goal chord, so doubling it is natural.
why 6 1/2 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and alto fall E–D and A–G a fifth apart, the alto an eighth earlier. Staggered like this Bach accepts them; strict exercises still count them.
why 6 5/8 LEAPS The alto walks down B♭–A–G and skips to E, then rises to F♯ for the fermata. The tritone B♭–E is spread over a line of several notes.
why 6 3/4 SEVENTH At the fermata the tenor's D, the root of the D chord, drops to A, its fifth. The D was never a seventh: the chord over the bass's D is D major in the making.
why 8 1/2 DOUBLING The same pattern as bar 6, a step lower: over the bass's C the upper voices pass through F toward the C major chord at the fermata. C is the goal chord's root, natural to double.
why 8 1/2 PARALLEL_FIFTHS As in bar 6, soprano and alto fall a fifth apart with the alto an eighth early. Bach repeats the pattern in sequence.
why 8 3/4 SEVENTH As in bar 6, the tenor's C is the root of the coming C chord, not a seventh; at the fermata it drops to G, the fifth.
why 9 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps up an augmented fourth, D–G♯, to put the dominant in first inversion with the leading tone in the bass. A sharp, expressive leap; G♯ reaches A two eighths later.
why 10 1/2 MISSING_THIRD The alto holds D over the A chord, a suspension that resolves by way of B to C♯ at the fermata. The third is only delayed, not missing.
why 10 1/2 LEAPS The bass resolves D♯ up to E and then leaps a fourth to A. The tritone D♯–A spans two chords.
